  "conclusions": [
    "batch=1 beats batch=16 at every depth: depth 10 is 113.8ms vs 166.3ms P50. Two threads extract little parallelism from a batch while padding to the longest member wastes compute on every shorter passage. Same effect as the 1.46x length-sorted batching win measured for the offline embedder in Phase 2.",
    "This makes the reproducible configuration (ISSUES.md I24) also the fast one, so there is no accuracy-vs-speed trade to make on batch size.",
    "max_tokens is not a useful lever: 256 -> 128 saves ~2% at depth 10 because passages are p50 ~77 tokens and truncation rarely binds. Kept at 256 to avoid truncating the long tail for no measurable gain.",
    "Cost is roughly linear in depth: ~11.4ms per candidate at batch=1.",
    "Assumption A2 ('cross-encoder rerank of 20 candidates fits in 45ms') is FALSE. Depth 20 costs 249.1ms P50, 5.5x the assumption, on a faster CPU than the deploy target."
